## Model Context Protocol (MCP)

✍️ Please read [my technical blog article about MCP here](https://faafospecialist.substack.com/p/claude-code-solution-to-use-mcp-servers).

### Pre-requisites

In ClaudeKit, you need to setup the MCP servers in `.claude/.mcp.json` file.

Copy the example file:
```bash
mv .claude/.mcp.json.example .claude/.mcp.json
```

Then add your MCP servers, below are some examples:

### [Context7](https://github.com/upstash/context7)
```json
{
   "mcpServers": {
      "context7": {
         "command": "npx",
         "args": ["-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp", "--api-key", "YOUR_API_KEY"],
      }
   }
}
```

### [Human MCP](https://github.com/mrgoonie/human-mcp/)

Human MCP is included in `.claude/.mcp.json.example`, but ClaudeKit disables it by default in `.claude/settings.json` because it requires a Gemini API key. To use it, set `GOOGLE_GEMINI_API_KEY` and remove `human-mcp` from `disabledMcpjsonServers`.

```json
{
   "mcpServers": {
      "human-mcp": {
         "command": "npx",
         "args": ["-y", "@goonnguyen/human-mcp@latest"],
         "env": { "GOOGLE_GEMINI_API_KEY": "YOUR_API_KEY" }
      }
   }
}
```

### [Chrome DevTools MCP](https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp)
```json
{
   "mcpServers": {
      "chrome-devtools": {
         "command": "npx",
         "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest"]
      }
   }
}
```

### Chrome Profile Browser Workflow

Use `ck:chrome-profile` when a task needs the user's actual Google Chrome profile. It opens URLs in the user's real Chrome profile, then the agent selects that tab through Chrome DevTools MCP.

Use it when browser automation needs real cookies, account state, workspaces, tenants, or a specific Chrome profile:

```bash
bash .claude/skills/chrome-profile/scripts/install.sh
chrome-profile doctor
chrome-profile setup
chrome-profile open --json work "https://github.com/your-org/your-repo/pulls"
```

If `doctor` reports no readable bridge, first try a live Chrome DevTools MCP page-list/read probe. The first Chrome DevTools MCP call may trigger Chrome's remote-control approval prompt; after the user approves, retry once before blocking. If both `doctor` and the live probe fail, set up one of these:

1. Configure `chrome-devtools-mcp` with auto-connect, then restart the agent session and approve Chrome's remote-control prompt if shown.
2. Or attach `chrome-devtools-mcp` to a Chrome remote-debugging endpoint:

```json
{
   "mcpServers": {
      "chrome-devtools": {
         "command": "npx",
         "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest", "--browserUrl", "http://127.0.0.1:9222"]
      }
   }
}
```

Before choosing a browser tool, decide whether the task needs a specific real Chrome profile: cookies, login/account state, workspace, tenant, or isolated profile key. If it does not, use `ck:agent-browser`, `ck:web-testing`, project-native Playwright/Vitest/Cypress, or Chrome DevTools MCP normal navigation. If it does, do not use MCP `new_page` or raw `navigate_page` as the opening/navigation path. Use `chrome-profile open --json <key> <url>`, then select the page whose URL contains the returned `bind_selector` such as `cdp-open=<token>`. Verify the selected URL also contains `cdp-profile=<key>`. If a live MCP probe proves `doctor` is a false negative, add `--force` only for that launch.

